<v NARRATOR>This drama uses an AI-generated voice. Voiced via Gemini 2.5.

3
00:00:14.861 --> 00:00:24.632
<v NARRATOR>Glance is a small arctic-fox-tween in a thick blue scarf, sitting very still on a winter log.

4
00:00:24.632 --> 00:00:34.643
<v NARRATOR>Above his head floats a speech bubble. The top half contains one word: "Cold." The bottom half is blank.

5
00:00:34.643 --> 00:00:44.374
<v NARRATOR>Beside it, a fainter ghost-text reads: "I do not want to talk right now, but I want you to stay."

6
00:00:44.374 --> 00:00:53.145
<v GLANCE>My name is Glance. I am the subtext-keeper. My speech bubble always has two layers.

7
00:00:53.145 --> 00:01:01.156
<v GLANCE>What I say is the surface text. What I mean is the ghost-text — the subtext underneath.

8
00:01:01.156 --> 00:01:10.407
<v GLANCE>Most real dialogue works this way. People say one thing and mean another. The two together are the whole conversation.

9
00:01:10.407 --> 00:01:18.578
<v NARRATOR>Patter the writing coach saw that Glance *was* the principle. He invited him to his workshop.

10
00:01:18.578 --> 00:01:24.829
<v GLANCE>I would have to bring my scarf. It is cold even indoors.

11
00:01:24.829 --> 00:01:33.240
<v NARRATOR>Now Glance sits in the workshop, demonstrating. He shows the surface bubble and the ghost-text for every line of dialogue.

12
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:43.491
<v NARRATOR>Well-written dialogue carries both layers. The explicit words are only half the communication; the implied meaning is the other half.

13
00:01:43.491 --> 00:02:00.302
<v GLANCE>The said is half. The meant is the other half. The reader will read both.

14
00:02:00.302 --> 00:02:09.073
<v NARRATOR>The surface is what's spoken. The subtext is what's meant. That is the whole dialogue.

15
